    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Cauchy sequences\n\nSometimes you can't *name* the limit but can still prove convergence: a sequence is **Cauchy** if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there's $N$ such that $|a_m - a_n| < \\varepsilon$ whenever $m, n \\ge N$. In $\\mathbb{R}$, a sequence is Cauchy iff it converges — that's the **completeness** of the reals.\n\nThis matters because most useful sequences (e.g. partial sums of a series) are easy to verify as Cauchy without first guessing the limit.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nEvery theorem in real analysis ultimately reduces to ε-N reasoning. Continuity (next notebook) is \"small change in input → small change in output\", differentiation is \"limit of slopes of secants\", integration is \"limit of Riemann sums\". All limits.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nAbbott, S. (2015) *Understanding Analysis*. 2nd edn. New York, NY: Springer New York. doi:[10.1007/978-1-4939-2712-8](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4939-2712-8) — ch. 2, *Sequences and Series*.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
